\section{Methods}{

Take a case with a missing onset, reported on day \eqn{t}, with onset day
\eqn{s} and delay \eqn{d = t - s}.
Let \eqn{T} be the data cut-off and \eqn{g(d)} the pooled empirical delay
pmf of complete cases.

Back-sampling (\code{backsampling}) uses \eqn{P(s \mid t) = g(t - s)}.

The mean shift (\code{mean}) uses
\deqn{s = t - \mathrm{round}\left(\sum_d d \, g(d)\right),}
with halves rounded up.

The forward draw (\code{fwd}) uses \eqn{P(s \mid t) = f(t - s)} for a given
forward delay \eqn{f}.

EM back-projection (\code{em}) uses
\eqn{P(s \mid t) \propto \lambda(s) f(t - s)}, with the onset curve
\eqn{\lambda} and a static delay \eqn{f} estimated jointly, allowing for
right truncation at \eqn{T}.

All methods assume onsets are missing at random given the report day.
